#!/bin/sh
#
# Prettier over the staged files, a stale-stamp warning, and an opt-in CRAP
# print. Three steps, and ONLY THE FIRST MAY FAIL THE COMMIT.
#
# ⚠️ **The gates are the contract and this hook is not one of them.** Every
# check here already refuses in CI — `pnpm format:check` in the `style` job, G56
# at merge — so this buys latency and no new coverage: a slip found in three
# seconds locally instead of three minutes in CI. That is worth having, and a
# contributor whose hook is broken, skipped or absent still passes every gate.
# See CONTRIBUTING.md and docs/adr/0083-hooks-arrive-with-pnpm-install.md.
#
# ⚠️ `--no-verify` skips all of it, and that is not a defect. Nothing here is a
# guarantee, so nothing here needs to be unskippable.
#
# ⚠️ **`pnpm lint` is deliberately NOT here.** `eslint.lint.config.mjs` sets
# `projectService: true`, so the parser loads the whole TypeScript program and
# the run costs about 7.3 seconds whatever subset of files it is given — a
# staged subset does not make it cheaper. Seven seconds on every commit is how a
# hook becomes the thing everyone clears with `--no-verify`. It runs pre-push
# instead, where it blocks.

# ── 1. Prettier over the staged files. The one step that may refuse. ────────
#
# ⚠️ The globs in package.json's `lint-staged` block name extensions rather than
# `*`, and that is load-bearing twice over. Prettier has no parser for `.astro`
# and ERRORS on one named explicitly — a directory sweep skips it silently, so
# `*` would fail every commit touching the site. And `.prettierignore` excludes
# Markdown and `fixtures/`, each for a measured reason recorded in that file;
# both exclusions were verified to hold for explicitly-named paths, with a
# positive control, rather than assumed from the file existing.
pnpm exec lint-staged || exit 1

# ── 2. The floors stamp. Warns, never refuses. ──────────────────────────────
#
# ⚠️ **`--check`, never a write.** A hook that rewrote `stryker.floors.json`
# here would change what is about to be committed, behind the author's back and
# outside the staged set.
#
# ⚠️ **This is a courtesy and NOT the guarantee.** G56 (`config-hash`) refuses
# the merge, and it has to stay the thing that does: Dependabot commits through
# the GitHub API with no working tree, so no git hook ever runs on a bot pull
# request, and any author clears this with `--no-verify`.
pnpm mutation:stamp --check >/dev/null 2>&1 || printf '\nstamp: stryker.floors.json disagrees with stryker.config.mjs. `pnpm mutation:stamp` writes it.\n       Not blocking this commit — G56 will refuse the pull request.\n'

# ── 3. The CRAP print. Opt-in per clone, advisory, never blocks. ────────────
#
# ⚠️ **This used to live in `.githooks/pre-commit`, which is retired**, because
# `core.hooksPath` is ONE SLOT and husky claims it for every contributor on
# `pnpm install`. That file's own header predicted this collision — *"a husky or
# lefthook install among the possibilities"* — and opting it in after this
# landed would take the slot back and lose steps 1 and 2. So the print moved in
# here rather than competing, and stayed opt-in rather than becoming everyone's.
#
# Opt in with:  git config --bool stacks.hooks.crap true
# Opt out with: git config --unset stacks.hooks.crap
#
# It prints a ranking whose exponents nobody ever calibrated; blocking a commit
# on one would be the refusing hook the spec turned down. Every failure — no
# install, no tsx, a Vitest run that died — costs the print and nothing else.
if [ "$(git config --bool stacks.hooks.crap)" = "true" ]; then
  pnpm exec tsx scripts/crap.ts || printf '\nCRAP: no print this commit — the step could not run.\n'
fi

exit 0
